WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:20.880
As you wander through the aisles of the grocery
store, you might find your attention caught

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.440
on any number of things.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:23.507
Frozen pizza.

00:00:23.507 --> 00:00:24.342
Cupcakes.

00:00:24.342 --> 00:00:25.419
Wine.

00:00:25.419 --> 00:00:30.539
And as delicious as all of those things are,
we doubt that any of them undergoes as spectacular

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:35.932
of a transformation as a packet of instant
yeast does when you shoot lasers at it.

00:00:37.087 --> 00:00:40.406
Because that is not how yeast always looks.

00:00:40.406 --> 00:00:43.040
In the packet, it’s dry and dull.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:45.648
And when you add water and look under a microscope—

00:00:45.648 --> 00:00:47.308
without any lasers added—

00:00:47.308 --> 00:00:52.039
yeast are more like stagnant, sturdy, living
bubbles.

00:00:52.039 --> 00:00:57.283
So what makes yeast shine so bright 
when hit with a laser?

00:00:57.283 --> 00:00:58.514
Well, we will get there.

00:00:58.514 --> 00:01:03.430
But first, let’s talk about the grocery
store, and why you can buy a packet full of

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:05.827
microbes while you are there.

00:01:05.827 --> 00:01:06.828
Because that is where James—

00:01:06.828 --> 00:01:10.074
our master of microscopes, our purveyor of ponds—

00:01:10.074 --> 00:01:11.570
got these yeast.

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:17.014
He bought them, in the form of instant yeast
that was sitting on the shelf of a supermarket.

00:01:17.014 --> 00:01:20.780
And that’s because for most of us, yeast
is an ingredient.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:25.004
It’s something a recipe calls for, like
milk or sugar.

00:01:25.004 --> 00:01:29.178
But yeast is also something much more than
an ingredient.

00:01:29.178 --> 00:01:35.000
It’s more like our ally, a friendly baker
on the inside who does so much of the work

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.360
for us, as long as we give it a few things
in exchange.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.708
When you buy a packet of instant yeast, what
you are really buying is a lot of

00:01:43.708 --> 00:01:45.687
Saccharomyces cerevisiae.

00:01:45.687 --> 00:01:51.457
That’s the name of the species, and it roughly
translates to “sweet fungus of beer”.

00:01:51.457 --> 00:01:53.559
But it’s known by many other names, like

00:01:53.559 --> 00:01:57.120
Brewer’s yeast, Baker’s yeast, and budding yeast.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:02.150
But those first two names are a tribute to
the role yeast play in helping to make our

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:06.823
beer, wine, and— what we’re going to 
focus on today— bread.

00:02:06.823 --> 00:02:11.690
If you’ve used instant yeast, you probably
conjure up the image of the tiny, brown-ish

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:16.390
white granules that fill the packet and froth
up when you add water.

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:21.940
Those granules are full of live yeast that
had been dried out for later use, and their

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:24.000
small size is important.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:29.710
It gives each of those minuscule yeast clumps
more surface area relative to their volume,

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:34.239
helping them dissolve faster when they are
mixed with liquid and hydrated into something

00:02:34.239 --> 00:02:36.720
that looks more like these cells.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.990
James is a master of microscopes, not a baker,
so he wasn’t planning to use these yeasts

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:43.309
to make bread.

00:02:43.309 --> 00:02:48.000
But yeast, of course, helps us make bread
using the same basic processes that helps

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.739
them survive.

00:02:49.739 --> 00:02:54.709
So the steps James took to bring these cells
to life can help us see some of the ingredients

00:02:54.709 --> 00:03:00.704
and processes that make our favorite loafs
possible, starting out with sugar.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:08.700
We often describe the size of microbes as
fractions of fractions of millimeters, but

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:12.470
it can be tough to wrap our minds around what
that really means.

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:17.819
Looking at these yeast cells dwarfed by a
few undissolved grains of sugar helps give

00:03:17.819 --> 00:03:19.617
a better sense of scale.

00:03:19.617 --> 00:03:25.069
Just think of how small a grain of sugar is
in your hand, and imagine just how much tinier

00:03:25.069 --> 00:03:27.492
a little yeast must be.

00:03:27.492 --> 00:03:32.500
Again, it’s easy to think of sugar and yeast
as two ingredients in bread, one added after

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:34.970
to make the right bit of chemistry happen.

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:39.196
And while that’s true, there’s also a
little more going on.

00:03:39.196 --> 00:03:44.580
Sugar is like a bribe that helps kickstart
our yeast friends into action, giving them

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:47.560
the sustenance they need to get moving.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:53.560
As the yeast feeds, they begin to divide,
forming smaller daughter cells that bud off

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:55.000
from their mothers.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:00.608
You can see some of those buds here, forming
ovals that branch off the circular mother cell.

00:04:00.608 --> 00:04:05.480
This is where the name “budding yeast”
comes from, and it’s yet another way that

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:08.173
yeast is different from our other ingredients.

00:04:08.173 --> 00:04:15.069
Sugar doesn’t make more sugar, but for yeast
replication is one of their objectives, driving

00:04:15.069 --> 00:04:18.632
the growth and survival of their population.

00:04:18.632 --> 00:04:24.293
But as the yeast accomplishes their goals, they 
happen to get some of our goals done as well.

00:04:24.293 --> 00:04:31.943
As the yeast digests sugar, they produce byproducts:
various organic acids, ethanol, and carbon dioxide— 

00:04:31.943 --> 00:04:35.720
which you can see here in bubbles expanding

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:38.510
and pushing against the field of yeast.

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:42.670
We are watching these bubbles grow in water,
and over the next few minutes, their edges

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:49.600
will push further and further out until these
two bubbles seem to take over the screen.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:54.460
But in bread dough, those carbon dioxide bubbles
cause the entire structure around the yeast

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:57.732
to expand and to rise.

00:04:57.732 --> 00:05:03.300
And when the dough is moved to the oven, the
carbon dioxide will shape the bread that forms.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:08.290
The ethanol byproduct isn’t visible like
those carbon dioxide bubbles are, but in bread

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:13.566
dough, the evaporation of ethanol also helps
the bread rise in the oven.

00:05:13.566 --> 00:05:17.706
And it will also shape the flavor of the bread
that comes out.

00:05:17.706 --> 00:05:23.039
While it is making bread for us, the world
inside the yeast is busy as well.

00:05:23.039 --> 00:05:26.410
Buried in dough, it’s impossible to see
what that means.

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:30.760
But microscopes are powerful tools when it
comes to digging up seemingly invisible forms

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:35.030
of beauty, especially when they have lasers
in them.

00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:40.500
With a microscope that sends a laser of UV
light straight at the yeast, molecules inside

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:46.569
the organism begin to fluoresce, a display
called autofluorescence that lights up the

00:05:46.569 --> 00:05:53.789
whole screen with rings of red and green and
yellow that press up against each other and overlap.

00:05:53.789 --> 00:05:58.700
To scientists who know how to crack the code,
those colors create a map of the molecules

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:00.620
inside the cell.

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:05.900
Those colors are actually the amino acids
and vitamins that make the yeast work, rising

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:10.600
and falling throughout the yeast’s life
cycle to provide the organism with what it

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:12.516
needs to survive.

00:06:12.516 --> 00:06:17.889
It’s as if someone turned on the lights
at the factory, illuminating all the processes

00:06:17.889 --> 00:06:20.509
that make the final product possible.

00:06:20.509 --> 00:06:25.150
And if we could dedicate a whole episode to
the stunning autofluorescing patterns of yeast,

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:26.228
we would.

00:06:26.228 --> 00:06:28.220
But there is one big challenge.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:32.088
You may have noticed that the videos we’ve
been showing of this are pretty short.

00:06:32.088 --> 00:06:36.248
Most only last a few seconds before we switch
to another video.

00:06:36.248 --> 00:06:42.237
And that is because those bright lights quickly
fade after being struck with the laser.

00:06:42.237 --> 00:06:47.067
Let’s sit here and watch this group of yeast,
initially bright red and green.

00:06:47.067 --> 00:06:50.522
After only a few seconds, the red is already
gone.

00:07:03.342 --> 00:07:08.970
And as more seconds pass, the green becomes
more and more faint, like someone has been

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:14.506
turning the brightness on your screen down
at an agonizingly slow pace.

00:07:35.184 --> 00:07:40.990
After a full minute, the screen has gone completely
black, with no hint at the vivid scene painted

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:46.411
across it so recently— almost as if it had never 
been there to begin with.

00:07:46.411 --> 00:07:50.750
But even as the lights fade, the factory remains.

00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:55.560
Every day, perhaps in your kitchen or in the
bakery around the corner from you, there is

00:07:55.560 --> 00:08:03.150
a group of invisible budding bakers occupied
with turning sugar into carbon dioxide and ethanol.

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:08.393
However transient their lives or their work,
the legacy they leave…well,

00:08:08.393 --> 00:08:10.469
there’s no arguing with that.

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:24.050
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:28.440
The people you're seeing on the screen right
now, those are our Patreon patrons.

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:32.700
They're the ones who make it possible for
us to get the yeast packets which aren't that

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:37.952
expensive, but also so that we have access
to the lasers, which are actually not that cheap.

00:08:37.952 --> 00:08:42.793
If you would like to become a patron on Patreon,
you can go to Patreon.com/JourneytoMicro.

00:08:42.793 --> 00:08:46.870
There you can get a bunch of cool perks and
